<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lubawa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lubawa, in German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a>, is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in present-day Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s conquest it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a>, who had a castle there and ruled the surrounding district as their own territory. With the rest of the Culm diocese it passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> in 1466.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lubawa.jpg" alt="Lubawa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zbigniew.czernik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lubawa_-_fontanna_na_Rynku_-_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lubawa lies on the Sandela River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>, in Iława County, about 18 kilometres south-east of Iława and some 115 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. It stands at an altitude of 145 metres. Its German name was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a> in Westpreußen and its Old Prussian name Lūbawa. The town is a member of the Cittaslow network.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1214 the local Prussian landlord Surwabuno was christened by Christian of Oliva, the first Catholic bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, who now appears on the town's coat of arms. Lubawa was first mentioned in a papal bull of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> dated 18 January 1216, and a wooden castle was built soon afterwards. Within the state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a> was created in 1243 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a>, and in 1257 the town became church property and the seat of the bishops of Culm. The castle was destroyed in 1268. Between 1301 and 1326 Bishop Arnold built a new one in stone, which Lithuanian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a> destroyed in 1330 and which was rebuilt.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> in 1410 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> captured the town, but it returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> when the war ended. In 1440 it joined the anti-Teutonic Prussian Confederation, at whose request King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> signed the act of incorporation of the region and town into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1454. Pro-Polish troops took the castle at the start of the Thirteen Years' War, and the Second Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> confirmed the incorporation in 1466. A great fire razed the town in 1533. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s castle several times between 1535 and 1539, and the decision to publish his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">De revolutionibus orbium coelestium</a> was made at Lubawa. In 1627 the castle became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palace of Bishop Jakub Zadzik.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed the town in 1772 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a>. It belonged to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> during the Napoleonic Wars, then returned to Prussia, and in 1871 became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. The 1890 German census counted 4,593 inhabitants, of whom 2,300 were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. It returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied the town after the invasion of 1939, and from 1939 to 1945 it kept a secret camp for Polish children and teenagers aged 10 to 17, who were subjected to forced labour, beatings and malnutrition. A monument to them stands in the town, which has been Polish since 1945. Lubawa is a centre of the furniture industry and of local tourism. Surviving landmarks include the ruins of a Gothic castle, two 15th-century towers, parts of the 14th-century city walls and the Gothic St. Anne's Church of 1330.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lubawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culm covered the Chełmno land, which Konrad of Masovia had granted to the Teutonic Order, and the bishop received a third of the diocese as his own territory, chiefly around <b>Löbau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-culm">Bishopric of Culm</a></li><li><blockquote>The land of <b>Lubawa</b> lay in the south-west of Prussia, next to the Chełmno land and Masovia.</blockquote><a href="/state/lubavians">Lubavians</a></li></ul></section>
